# Styling and accessibility

- Use CSS Modules for component styles and CSS custom properties for design tokens; do not introduce Tailwind.
- Organize tokens as primitives, semantic roles, then component variables. Components consume semantic roles.
- Limit global CSS to tokens, font setup, reset, document defaults, and deliberate Payload theme integration.
- Do not target generated class names or fragile DOM ancestry. Document any necessary upstream override.
- Support 1440px, 1024px, 390px, and fluid widths between them without clipped actions or hidden content.
- Meet WCAG AA contrast. Never communicate status, errors, or selection by color alone.
- Every interactive element needs visible hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, and loading states where relevant.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; animation must clarify state and never block work.
- Use a Cyrillic-capable, legally usable font with a resilient system fallback stack.
- Keep touch targets at least 44px where practical and preserve zoom and text resizing.
- Tables need a deliberate narrow-screen strategy; do not silently remove columns containing required decisions.
- Test keyboard navigation, focus trapping/restoration, form announcements, and meaningful accessible names.
